  <section>
    <h2 id="entry-rules">Do International Visitors Need a Visa for CIIE 2026?</h2>

    <p><strong>It depends on your passport and your itinerary.</strong></p>

    <p>
      There is no single visa rule that applies to every international CIIE visitor. In general, your trip will fall into one of three broad categories:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Chinese visa required:</strong> you do not qualify for an applicable visa-free policy, or your itinerary does not meet the conditions of the policy you hoped to use.</li>
      <li><strong>Visa-free entry under another policy:</strong> your passport and trip qualify under a current Chinese visa-exemption arrangement that does not depend on transit.</li>
      <li><strong>240-hour visa-free transit:</strong> you are an eligible traveler genuinely transiting Mainland China on the way to a different country or region and you satisfy the other published requirements.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      China currently applies the 240-hour visa-free transit policy to nationals of <strong>57 countries</strong>. Eligible travelers may enter through designated ports, including ports in Shanghai, and may stay within the permitted areas for no more than 240 hours while traveling onward to a third country or region.
    </p>

    <p class="cta">
      <a href="/china-240-hour-visa-free-transit/#checker">
        Check your CIIE itinerary with our 240-hour transit eligibility checker &rarr;
      </a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Can You Attend CIIE Using China's 240-Hour Visa-Free Transit Policy?</h2>

    <p>
      Potentially, yes. The current policy allows eligible travelers to undertake short-term activities including <strong>business</strong>, so attending a trade exhibition can be compatible with the policy if the rest of your trip qualifies.
    </p>

    <p>
      The most important routing rule is that you must be transiting Mainland China on the way to a <strong>different country or region</strong>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Think of the route as:
    </p>

    <p><strong>Country or Region A &rarr; Mainland China &rarr; Country or Region B</strong></p>

    <p>
      A and B are the country or region immediately before and immediately after Mainland China, and must be different. Layovers can change which places count as A and B. You also need an ordinary passport, an onward ticket with a confirmed seat and departure date, and must meet the nationality, port, permitted-area and stay-length requirements.
    </p>

    <p class="table-hint">On narrow screens, scroll the itinerary table sideways to read all columns.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap route-table" role="region" aria-label="Transit itinerary examples" tabindex="0">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Example itinerary</th>
            <th>Route test</th>
            <th>Why</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>United States &rarr; Shanghai &rarr; Japan</td>
            <td><strong>May qualify</strong></td>
            <td>The country before China and the country after China are different.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>United Kingdom &rarr; Shanghai &rarr; Hong Kong</td>
            <td><strong>May qualify</strong></td>
            <td>Hong Kong is treated separately from Mainland China for the transit-route test.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Canada &rarr; Shanghai &rarr; South Korea</td>
            <td><strong>May qualify</strong></td>
            <td>Canada and South Korea are different countries.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Germany &rarr; Shanghai &rarr; Singapore</td>
            <td><strong>May qualify</strong></td>
            <td>Germany and Singapore are different countries.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>United States &rarr; Shanghai &rarr; United States</td>
            <td><strong>Generally does not qualify</strong></td>
            <td>The traveler returns to the same country rather than continuing to a different country or region.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Japan &rarr; Shanghai &rarr; Japan</td>
            <td><strong>Generally does not qualify</strong></td>
            <td>Japan is immediately before and after Mainland China.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      These examples evaluate only the transit-route requirement. A route that passes this test is not automatically eligible for 240-hour visa-free transit.
    </p>

