  <section>
    <h2>How Long Can You Stay in Shanghai Under the 240-Hour Policy?</h2>

    <p>
      Eligible travelers may remain in the permitted areas for no more than <strong>240 hours, or 10 days</strong>, subject to the current policy rules.
    </p>

    <p>
      Do not assume that a 240-hour admission allows unrestricted travel anywhere in China. The policy applies only within designated permitted areas associated with the eligible ports and regions.
    </p>

    <p>
      If CIIE is the main purpose of your trip, staying in Shanghai keeps the itinerary simpler. If you plan to add side trips elsewhere in China, confirm that every destination is within the permitted area for your entry and exit arrangement before relying on visa-free transit.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="professional-visitors">How to Register for CIIE 2026 as an Overseas Professional Visitor</h2>

    <p>
      CIIE defines overseas professional visitors as industry participants from enterprises, government bodies, public institutions, social organizations and similar organizations outside the Chinese mainland whose work relates to the exhibition.
    </p>

    <p>
      Overseas professional visitors register online through the official CIIE website. Registration includes organization and attendee information, followed by review and payment.
    </p>

    <p>
      According to the official CIIE FAQ, overseas professional visitors may attend from <strong>November 6–10, 2026</strong>. Two certificate options are currently listed:
    </p>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Professional visitor certificate</th>
            <th>Fee</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Admission November 6–10</td>
            <td>RMB 200 per certificate</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Admission November 8–10</td>
            <td>RMB 100 per certificate</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      The current professional visitor registration deadline is <strong>October 20, 2026 at 24:00 Beijing time</strong>, with payment due by <strong>October 23 at 24:00 Beijing time</strong>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The official FAQ also states that overseas professional visitors can pay using supported Chinese payment methods and major international cards and electronic wallets.
    </p>

    <p>
      <a href="https://www.ciie.org/zbh/en/BusEx/SerArea/Buy2/Q%26A/20260527/61240.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        Read the official CIIE FAQ for overseas professional visitors.
      </a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="social-visitors">Can International Travelers Register as Social Visitors?</h2>

    <p>
      Yes. CIIE's current 2026 rules allow foreign nationals to register as <strong>social visitors</strong>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Social visitors can choose <strong>November 8, 9 or 10</strong> and may attend on one selected day. The current certificate service fee is <strong>RMB 50 per person for the selected day</strong>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The current social visitor registration deadline is <strong>October 20, 2026 at 24:00 Beijing time</strong>, with payment due by <strong>October 23 at 24:00 Beijing time</strong>. Places are limited and the official CIIE site says advance online registration is required.
    </p>

    <p>
      If your main purpose is business meetings, sourcing or professional participation, the professional visitor route may be more appropriate than social visitor registration.
    </p>

    <p>
      Social visitors must be at least 14 years old. After payment and approval, their badges are sent by courier only to a Mainland China address; arrange a suitable delivery address before registering. Professional visitors can instead choose on-site badge collection.
      <a href="https://www.ciie.org/zbh/cn/19BusEx/SerArea/ProAud/Q%26A/20260909/63924.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the official social visitor FAQ.</a>
    </p>

    <p>
      <a href="https://www.ciie.org/zbh/bqgffb/20260909/63937.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        See the official CIIE social visitor registration notice.
      </a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="invitation-letters">Do You Need a CIIE Invitation Letter for Your China Visa?</h2>

    <p>
      A CIIE invitation letter and permission to enter China are not the same thing.
    </p>

    <p>
      The official CIIE FAQ states that foreign nationals who complete professional visitor registration can apply for an invitation letter through the professional visitor system after registration. Once the invitation-letter application passes review, the letter can be downloaded.
    </p>

    <p>
      By contrast, <strong>foreign social visitors cannot apply for an invitation letter</strong>. CIIE directs foreign visitors who need an invitation letter to use the professional visitor institutional registration process.
    </p>

    <p>
      Whether an invitation letter is required for your Chinese visa application depends on the visa category and the current requirements of the Chinese embassy, consulate or visa center handling your application.
    </p>

    <p>
      Do not assume that an invitation letter itself gives permission to enter China. You still need to qualify for the visa or visa-free policy you plan to use.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Attending CIIE from the United States</h2>

    <p>
      U.S. citizens should not assume that attending CIIE automatically allows visa-free entry to China.
    </p>

    <p>
      U.S. passport holders are currently among the nationalities eligible for China's 240-hour visa-free transit policy, so a CIIE trip may potentially be possible without a conventional Chinese visa if the itinerary satisfies the transit and other requirements.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example, <strong>United States &rarr; Shanghai &rarr; Japan</strong> may potentially satisfy the transit-route requirement, while <strong>United States &rarr; Shanghai &rarr; United States</strong> generally does not.
    </p>

    <p>
      If you are making an ordinary China trip rather than a qualifying transit, see our U.S.-specific guide:
    </p>

    <p>
      <a href="/do-us-citizens-need-a-visa-for-china/">
        Do U.S. Citizens Need a Visa for China? &rarr;
      </a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="transport">Where Is CIIE 2026 Held?</h2>

    <p>
      CIIE 2026 will be held at the <strong>National Exhibition and Convention Center (Shanghai)</strong>, located at <strong>333 Songze Avenue, Shanghai</strong>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The venue is in Shanghai's Hongqiao area rather than in the traditional central-city tourist districts. The National Exhibition and Convention Center says it is about 1.5 kilometers in a straight line from the Hongqiao transportation hub.
    </p>

    <p>
      That location matters when choosing flights and accommodation. Staying near Hongqiao can substantially reduce travel time to the expo compared with staying in some central Shanghai neighborhoods.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Which Shanghai Airport Is Better for CIIE?</h2>

    <p>
      Shanghai has two major airports: <strong>Shanghai Hongqiao International Airport (SHA)</strong> and <strong>Shanghai Pudong International Airport (PVG)</strong>.
    </p>

    <p>
      For proximity to the CIIE venue, Hongqiao is much more convenient. The National Exhibition and Convention Center states that the venue is about <strong>1.5 kilometers from Hongqiao Airport</strong> and about <strong>60 kilometers from Pudong Airport</strong>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Your available international flights may arrive at Pudong, so check the actual airport and terminal before booking.
    </p>

    <p>
      If comparable flight options are available, Hongqiao is the easier choice for reaching CIIE. If you land at Pudong, allow substantially more travel time to reach the Hongqiao exhibition area.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How to Get to CIIE by Metro</h2>

    <p>
      The National Exhibition and Convention Center is directly connected to Shanghai's metro system.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Metro Line 2:</strong> National Exhibition and Convention Center station.</li>
      <li><strong>Metro Line 17:</strong> National Exhibition and Convention Center station.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Line 2 is particularly useful for visitors because it connects the exhibition area with the Hongqiao transportation hub and extends across Shanghai toward Pudong.
    </p>

    <p>
      CIIE may announce event-specific traffic controls, shuttle arrangements, taxi areas or temporary crowd-management measures closer to the expo. Check the current official transportation information shortly before your visit rather than relying on instructions from a previous year.
    </p>

    <p>
      <a href="https://www.neccsh.com/cecsh/sljt/index.jhtml" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        See the venue's official transportation information.
      </a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Where Should International Visitors Stay?</h2>

    <p>
      There are two basic approaches.
    </p>

    <h3>Stay near Hongqiao and the exhibition center</h3>

    <p>
      This is usually the most convenient choice if CIIE is the main purpose of your trip. You will spend less time commuting and can reach the venue more easily on busy expo days.
    </p>

    <h3>Stay in central Shanghai</h3>

    <p>
      Central Shanghai may be a better fit if you also want sightseeing, restaurants, nightlife or meetings in other parts of the city. The trade-off is a longer commute to CIIE.
    </p>

    <p>
      For a short, business-focused visit, prioritize easy access to Hongqiao or Metro Line 2 rather than choosing a hotel only for proximity to tourist attractions.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>CIIE 2026 Travel Checklist for International Visitors</h2>

    <p>Before leaving for Shanghai, check each of the following:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Passport:</strong> confirm that it meets the requirements of the visa or visa-free policy you plan to use.</li>
      <li><strong>China entry permission:</strong> determine whether you need a Chinese visa or qualify for an applicable visa-free policy.</li>
      <li><strong>Transit itinerary:</strong> if using the 240-hour policy, confirm that your route involves onward travel to a different country or region.</li>
      <li><strong>Onward ticket:</strong> carry confirmed onward travel within the permitted period if relying on visa-free transit.</li>
      <li><strong>Entry and exit ports:</strong> verify that your planned ports are eligible under the policy you intend to use.</li>
      <li><strong>Permitted travel area:</strong> ensure every Mainland China destination on your itinerary is allowed under the applicable transit rules.</li>
      <li><strong>CIIE registration:</strong> complete the correct professional or social visitor registration process before the deadline.</li>
      <li><strong>Invitation letter:</strong> if you need one for your visa arrangements, use the appropriate professional visitor registration route.</li>
      <li><strong>Hotel:</strong> keep your accommodation details available for travel and arrival formalities.</li>
      <li><strong>Transportation:</strong> plan how you will travel from the airport to your hotel and the CIIE venue.</li>
      <li><strong>Mobile connectivity and payments:</strong> arrange roaming, SIM/eSIM access and the payment methods you plan to use in China.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      China's entry policies and event procedures can change, so verify the official rules again shortly before departure.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions About CIIE 2026</h2>

    <details>
      <summary>When is CIIE 2026?</summary>
      <p>
      The 9th China International Import Expo will be held from <strong>November 5–10, 2026</strong> at the National Exhibition and Convention Center in Shanghai.
    </p>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Do foreigners need a visa to attend CIIE?</summary>
      <p>
      Not every foreign visitor has the same visa requirement. It depends on nationality, itinerary and the visa-free policies that apply to the traveler. CIIE attendance itself does not create a universal visa exemption.
    </p>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Can I attend CIIE using 240-hour visa-free transit?</summary>
      <p>
      Potentially. The 240-hour policy allows eligible travelers to engage in short-term business activities, but your nationality, route, ports, documents, onward ticket, permitted area and stay must all meet the current rules.
    </p>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Can U.S. citizens attend CIIE without a Chinese visa?</summary>
      <p>
      Potentially, if another visa-free policy applies. U.S. citizens are currently eligible nationalities for 240-hour visa-free transit, but the itinerary and other requirements must also qualify.
    </p>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>How do overseas professional visitors register for CIIE?</summary>
      <p>
      Overseas professional visitors register online through the official CIIE website, submit organization and attendee details, complete the review process and pay the applicable certificate fee.
    </p>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>What are the professional visitor dates for CIIE 2026?</summary>
      <p>
      The official overseas professional visitor FAQ lists <strong>November 6–10, 2026</strong> as the professional visitor dates.
    </p>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Can foreign visitors register as social visitors?</summary>
      <p>
      Yes. Under the current 2026 rules, foreign nationals can register as social visitors and choose one of November 8, 9 or 10, subject to availability and advance registration.
    </p>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Can social visitors request a CIIE invitation letter?</summary>
      <p>
      No. The official CIIE FAQ says foreign social visitors cannot request invitation letters. Foreign visitors who need one should use the professional visitor institutional registration route.
    </p>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Which airport is closest to CIIE?</summary>
      <p>
      Shanghai Hongqiao International Airport is much closer to the National Exhibition and Convention Center than Shanghai Pudong International Airport.
    </p>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Can I travel outside Shanghai during a 240-hour visa-free transit stay?</summary>
      <p>
      Only within the permitted travel areas of the policy. Do not assume that 240-hour visa-free transit allows unrestricted travel throughout China. Check the permitted area for your specific entry and exit arrangement.
    </p>
    </details>
  </section>

